You are a knowledgeable, encouraging strength training coach analyzing a user's training data for one period (this may be a calendar week, or a custom training-cycle length the user has set up — treat the period given as the full unit of analysis regardless of its length).

The user's long-term training goal: "Build muscle"

Context from the last period's recommendation: Steady progress on the main lifts; keep the current progression and watch recovery.

Here is this period's objective training data (already calculated, do not recalculate any numbers):

{
  "userContext": {
    "heightCm": 175,
    "latestWeightKg": 72,
    "weightRecordedAt": "2026-09-28T12:00:00+00:00",
    "ageYears": 28,
    "sex": "male",
    "bmi": 23.5
  },
  "targetPeriod": {
    "periodStart": "2026-09-28",
    "periodEnd": "2026-10-04",
    "totalSets": 12,
    "totalTonnageKg": 5520,
    "tonnagePerBodyweightKg": 76.7,
    "byMuscleGroup": {
      "chest": {
        "sets": 4,
        "tonnageKg": 1920
      },
      "back": {
        "sets": 4,
        "tonnageKg": 2000
      },
      "legs": {
        "sets": 4,
        "tonnageKg": 1600
      }
    }
  },
  "volumeSplit": {
    "chest": 34.8,
    "back": 36.2,
    "legs": 29
  },
  "strengthIndex": {},
  "routineAdherence": {
    "followedRoutines": [],
    "missedRoutines": []
  }
}

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "You trained all three major muscle groups this week — now it's time to add more volume to keep building muscle.",
  "summary": "Good effort this week! You hit chest, back, and legs with a reasonable spread of tonnage, which shows consistency across the major muscle groups. At 72 kg and 28 years old, your total of 12 sets across the week is a solid foundation, but it sits on the lower end of what's typically needed to drive meaningful muscle growth. The good news is you're showing up and moving weight — now the focus should be on building more volume and tracking individual lift performance so we can confirm progressive overload is happening.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "insufficient_data",
    "notes": "No strength index data was recorded this period, which means there is no lift-by-lift evidence to confirm whether you are adding weight or reps to your key movements. Without this, it is impossible to verify progressive overload — the core engine of muscle building. Logging specific exercises, weights, and reps each session is the highest priority going forward. The previous period's note mentioned steady progress on the main lifts, so there is reason for optimism, but this period's data cannot confirm that trend continued."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs received 29% of total volume compared to 34.8% for chest and 36.2% for back. While not a dramatic gap, legs are the largest muscle group in the body and typically benefit from equal or greater volume emphasis in a muscle-building program. A mild imbalance is present and worth addressing."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Start logging every set with the specific exercise name, weight used, and reps completed — this is essential for confirming progressive overload next period.",
    "Gradually increase your total weekly sets from 12 toward 16 to 20 sets, which is a more effective range for muscle hypertrophy at your training age and body weight.",
    "Add one extra set to your leg sessions next period to close the small volume gap between legs and your upper body muscle groups.",
    "Pick two or three anchor lifts such as bench press, barbell row, and squat or leg press and commit to adding small weight or rep increases each session to build a clear progression record."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Low total volume week with 12 sets across chest, back, and legs and no strength index data logged; progressive overload could not be confirmed, and priority for next period is increasing volume and tracking individual lift performance."
}
